tel 1.0

base burdock
import soundness

info   slim application JARs: dependencies named by content hash, fetched, verified and cached at launch
detail
    _Burdock_ makes an application JAR small without making it fragile. A JAR which carries
    every dependency it needs ships tens of megabytes of library code, most of it already
    published somewhere public; Burdock replaces each published dependency with a reference to
    it, named by the SHA-256 of the exact bytes the compiler read, and a small bootstrap loader
    fetches, verifies and caches them when the application starts.

    The work happens in three stages:

    - **at compile time**, `externalize` wraps the application's entry point; its macro hashes
      every dependency JAR on the build's classpath, hard-links each into the Burdock cache,
      and embeds the list of hashes in the compiled output as the resource
      META-INF/burdock.deps;
    - **after the build**, the `soundness.repackage` entry point (the `repackage` method here)
      rewrites the JAR in place: each hash which resolves to a public URL — through the release
      assets of hinted GitHub repositories (`GitHub`) or through deps.dev (`DepsDev`) — becomes
      a `Burdock-Require` reference, and anything else is inlined from the cache;
    - **at launch**, `Bootstrap`, the one class the repackaged JAR adds, downloads every
      requirement not already cached, checks each against its hash, and starts the
      application on a classpath in manifest order.

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    import soundness.*

    @main
    def myApp(): Unit = externalize:
      Out.println(t"hello, world")
    ```

    ```sh
    java -cp app.jar:burdock.jar soundness.repackage --github propensive/soundness
    java -jar app.jar
    ```

    **The cache.** Dependency JARs are kept, by hash, as `~/.cache/soundness/jars/<sha256>.jar`.
    The `externalize` macro and the repackager use that directory under the user's home; the
    bootstrap honours `XDG_CACHE_HOME`, using `$XDG_CACHE_HOME/soundness/jars` when it is set
    and non-empty. Earlier releases used `~/.cache/burdock`; nothing migrates it, so the first
    launch after upgrading downloads every requirement again, and the old directory may be
    deleted.

    Because a reference names exact bytes, the completeness invariant holds: every dependency
    is either referenced or present, a hash which is neither stops the repackage with a
    `Repackager.RepackageError`, and a download whose checksum does not match stops the launch.

    The library has two JVM-only source sets: `burdock.core`, with `externalize`, the
    `Repackager` and its resolvers, which is exported to the `soundness` umbrella together with
    the `soundness.repackage` command-line entry point; and `burdock.boot`, the single Java
    class `Bootstrap`, which depends on nothing but the JDK so that it can run before any
    dependency has been fetched.

refer burdock.externalize
refer burdock.Repackager
refer burdock.Bootstrap
refer burdock.repackage

entry .externalize   wraps an application's entry point so that Burdock can externalize its dependencies
    `externalize(block)` returns `block` unchanged at runtime. Its macro, while the code
    compiles, reads the compiler's classpath, computes the SHA-256 of every dependency JAR on
    it, hard-links each into the cache at `~/.cache/soundness/jars/<sha256>.jar` (copying where
    the cache is on another filesystem), and writes the hashes, one per line, to the resource
    META-INF/burdock.deps in the compile output, so it is packaged into the JAR like any
    other resource.

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    import soundness.*

    @main
    def myApp(): Unit = externalize:
      Out.println(t"hello, world")
    ```

    Nothing else about the application changes: it is built, tested and run as an ordinary
    JAR, and the resource is inert until `repackage` reads it. Whether each dependency is
    published is decided later, by the repackager; the cache covers whatever cannot be
    resolved. The hard-links live on the build machine, so a JAR with unpublished dependencies
    must be repackaged where it was built.

entry .repackage   rewrites the running application's JAR in place, externalizing its published dependencies
  detail
      The command-line logic behind `soundness.repackage`, launched with the application JAR
      and Burdock on the classpath:

      ```sh
      java -cp app.jar:burdock.jar soundness.repackage --github owner/repo
      ```

      It locates the application JAR as the classpath entry holding META-INF/burdock.deps
      (raising `Repackager.UserError` if there is none, or if that entry is a directory), reads
      the `Bootstrap` class from Burdock's own JAR, builds a resolver which consults the
      hinted GitHub repositories before deps.dev, and calls `Repackager.repackage` into a
      temporary sibling, which then atomically replaces the original. A `GITHUB_TOKEN`
      environment variable, if set, lifts GitHub's unauthenticated rate limit.

      Only arguments of the form `--github owner/repo`, `--github=owner/repo` or a
      comma-separated list of either are accepted (see `parseArguments`). While resolving, a
      progress bar is drawn in place when standard output is a terminal, and suppressed when
      it is redirected; at the end a summary is printed: entries read and written, classes kept,
      inlined and stripped, the GitHub assets indexed, and each externalized requirement. Any
      error is printed and the process exits with status 1.

      This method is not exported to the `soundness` umbrella: the umbrella's own
      `soundness.repackage`, a `@main` method taking the arguments as `Text*`, is the entry
      point to launch, and calls `burdock.repackage`.
  refer burdock.Repackager
  refer burdock.parseArguments

entry .parseArguments   parses `--github` hints into GitHub repositories
    Accepts `--github owner/repo`, `--github=owner/repo`, and comma-separated lists in either
    form, any number of times. An unrecognized argument, a `--github` with no operand, or a
    repository not of the form owner/repo raises `Repackager.UserError` with a usage line,
    _usage: soundness.repackage [--github owner/repo]..._. It is not exported to the
    `soundness` umbrella, so it is reached as `burdock.parseArguments`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    burdock.parseArguments(List(t"--github", t"propensive/soundness,propensive/fuel"))
    ```

entry .Repackager   rewrites a JAR: published dependencies become references, others are inlined from the cache
  detail
      The repackager's core, free of I/O beyond reading the input JAR and writing the output.
      `.repackage` keeps the application's own classes, drops the bundled classes of every
      dependency it can resolve to a URL (listing them instead in the `Burdock-Require`
      manifest attribute), inlines the classes of the rest from the cache, adds the
      `Bootstrap` class, and rewrites the manifest so that `Main-Class` is `burdock.Bootstrap`
      and `Burdock-Main` the original entry point.

      The resolver, the cache lookup and the progress callback are injected, so the logic is
      testable without the network or the filesystem:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val published = url"https://repo1.maven.org/maven2/g/a/1/a-1.jar"
      val resolve: Repackager.Resolver = hash => if hash == t"aaa" then published else Unset
      val cached: Repackager.CacheReader = _ => (List[Zip.Entry](): List[Zip.Entry])

      val (requirements, inlined) = Repackager.partition(List(t"aaa", t"bbb"), resolve, cached)
      ```

      Entries are copied verbatim, still compressed, so a Stored entry stays Stored and a
      Deflated one keeps its CRC and compressed size; only the new manifest and the bootstrap
      class are compressed afresh. Directory entries are dropped, and where two entries share
      a name the first is kept: the force-included bootstrap over any bundled copy, and an
      application class over a cached one.
  refer burdock.Bootstrap
  refer burdock.DepsDev
  refer burdock.GitHub
  entry .partition   splits dependency hashes into requirements to reference and cached entries to inline
      `partition(hashes, resolve, cached, progress)` classifies every hash: one which `resolve`
      maps to a URL becomes a `Requirement`, one which `cached` finds contributes its entries,
      and one which is neither raises `Repackager.RepackageError`, _dependency `hash` is
      neither published nor cached_. Lookups run concurrently on virtual threads, sixteen at a
      time, and `progress` is called with `(completed, total)` after each group, on the calling
      thread. Results are in the order of `hashes`, which is the classpath order.
  entry .repackage   rewrites `inputJar` into `outputJar`, returning a `Repackager.Summary`
      Takes the input and output paths, a `Resolver`, a `CacheReader`, the bytes of the
      `Bootstrap` class and an optional `Progress` callback, and raises
      `Repackager.RepackageError` when the JAR has no manifest, no META-INF/burdock.deps
      resource or no `Main-Class`, when a dependency is neither published nor cached, and for
      any filesystem, stream or archive failure. Published dependencies found in the cache have
      their bundled entries stripped; one absent from the cache cannot be identified, so its
      entries stay bundled: still correct, only not slimmed.
  entry .Resolver   resolves a dependency hash to its public download URL, or `Unset`
      `Text => Optional[HttpUrl]`. The command line composes `GitHub.index` with
      `DepsDev.mavenUrl`.
  entry .Progress   a callback receiving `(completed, total)` after each group of dependencies is classified
  entry .CacheReader   looks a dependency hash up in the local cache, giving its entries or `Unset`
      `Text => Optional[List[Zip.Entry]]`. Directories and the manifest are excluded, and the
      payloads stay lazy, so stripping a published dependency's entries by name does not read
      the cached JAR's contents.
  entry .Requirement   an externalized dependency: its URL and the SHA-256 it must match
    entry #url   where the bootstrap downloads the dependency from
    entry #digest   the dependency's SHA-256, as lowercase hex
    entry #text   the requirement as it appears in `Burdock-Require`: `<sha256>:<url>`
  entry .Summary   what a repackage did, for the caller to report
    entry #inputEntries   how many entries the input JAR had
    entry #directoriesSkipped   how many directory entries were dropped
    entry #ownKept   how many of the application's own entries were kept
    entry #externalized   the requirements written to `Burdock-Require`, in classpath order
    entry #inlined   how many entries were inlined from cached dependencies
    entry #stripped   how many bundled entries of published dependencies were removed
    entry #outputEntries   how many entries the output JAR has, the manifest included
  entry .UserError   a mistake in how the repackager was invoked
    detail
        Raised for a JAR which was not built with Burdock (_this JAR was not built with Burdock
        (no META-INF/burdock.deps)_), one which is a directory rather than a file, a malformed
        argument or repository hint, and a failure to list a hinted repository's releases.
    entry #detail   what was wrong
  entry .RepackageError   a JAR which could not be repackaged
    detail
        Raised by `Repackager.repackage` and `Repackager.partition`: no manifest, no
        `Main-Class`, no META-INF/burdock.deps resource, a dependency neither published nor
        cached, or a filesystem, stream, archive or concurrency failure.
    entry #detail   what went wrong
  entry .BurdockMain   the `Burdock-Main` manifest attribute: the application's own entry point
  entry .BurdockRequire   the `Burdock-Require` manifest attribute: the externalized dependencies
  entry .BurdockVerbosity   the `Burdock-Verbosity` manifest attribute: how much the bootstrap prints
      One of `silent`, `error` (the default), `info` or `debug`; the repackager writes
      `silent`. Any other value stops the launch with status 2.
  entry .burdockMain   writes `Burdock-Main` as a fully-qualified class name
  entry .burdockRequire   writes `Burdock-Require` as space-separated `<sha256>:<url>` items
  entry .burdockVerbosity   writes `Burdock-Verbosity` as text

entry .Bootstrap   the runtime entry point of a repackaged JAR: fetches, verifies and loads its dependencies
  detail
      A Java class in `burdock.boot`, and the only class a repackaged JAR adds: it must run
      before any dependency is present, so it uses only the JDK, and compiles to a single class
      file. `java -jar app.jar` starts it as the manifest's `Main-Class`.

      It reads `Burdock-Require`, downloads every requirement not already in the cache —
      `$XDG_CACHE_HOME/soundness/jars` if that variable is set and non-empty, otherwise
      `~/.cache/soundness/jars` — up to eight at a time with connect and read timeouts, and
      checks each download's SHA-256 against the manifest's hash (and a cached file's too). It
      then builds a classloader over the JAR and the requirements, in manifest order, sets it
      as the thread's context classloader, and invokes `main` on the class `Burdock-Main`
      names.

      A checksum mismatch exits with status 1; a malformed `Burdock-Require` item, an invalid
      `Burdock-Verbosity`, a missing manifest or entry point, or any failure to launch exits
      with status 2. A launcher which supervises startup may set the system property
      `burdock.progress` to a file path: while fetching, the bootstrap keeps one line in it,
      `<completed> <total> <bytes>`, written atomically, and deletes it when done.
  entry .main   launches the application named by `Burdock-Main`, after fetching its requirements

entry .DepsDev   resolves a dependency's SHA-256 to its Maven Central URL through Google's deps.dev
  detail
      deps.dev ("Open Source Insights") indexes published artifacts by content hash. `.mavenUrl`
      asks its query API which Maven artifact has a hash and builds the artifact's URL on
      repo1.maven.org; anything deps.dev does not know as a Maven artifact is `Unset`, and the
      repackager then inlines the dependency from the cache instead. The case classes model the
      part of the API's JSON response which matters.
  entry .mavenUrl   the Maven Central URL of the JAR with a given SHA-256, or `Unset`
      Takes the hash as hex, sends it base64-encoded as the API expects, and picks the first
      result whose system is `MAVEN`. It logs `DepsEvent.Querying` at `fine` and
      `DepsEvent.Resolved` at `info`, so it needs a `DepsEvent is Loggable` in scope. Every
      failure — the network, an unexpected response, an unknown artifact — gives `Unset`.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import logging.silentLogging

      DepsDev.mavenUrl(t"a"*64)
      ```
  entry .Unresolved   a hash which is not a known Maven artifact
      _not a known Maven artifact_. Raised and caught within `.mavenUrl`, which answers `Unset`.
  entry .VersionKey   an artifact's identity in a deps.dev response
    entry #system   the package system, such as `MAVEN`
    entry #name   the artifact's name, `group:artifact` for Maven
    entry #version   the artifact's version
  entry .Version   one version in a deps.dev response
    entry #versionKey   the version's identity
  entry .Result   one match in a deps.dev response
    entry #version   the matching version
  entry .QueryResult   a deps.dev hash query's response
    entry #results   every artifact version with the hash

entry .GitHub   resolves dependency hashes against the release assets of hinted GitHub repositories
  detail
      GitHub keeps no global index by hash, but its REST API lists a repository's releases,
      and each asset carries a `digest` (`sha256:<hex>`). So the repositories named with
      `--github` are indexed up front — a few requests each, rather than one per dependency —
      and the index is consulted before deps.dev, since a repository the user named should take
      precedence.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val release = GitHub.Release(List(GitHub.Asset(t"lib-1.jar",
        t"https://github.com/o/r/releases/download/v1/lib-1.jar", t"sha256:${t"a"*64}")))

      GitHub.indexReleases(List(release))
      ```
  refer burdock.Repackager
  entry .Repository   a GitHub repository, as an owner and a name
    entry .parse   reads `owner/repo`, raising `Repackager.UserError` for anything else
        Exactly one slash, with both parts non-empty: `t"owner"`, `t"owner/"`, `t"/repo"` and
        `t"a/b/c"` are all rejected, with _`text` is not a GitHub repository of the form
        owner/repo_.
    entry .showable   shows a repository as `owner/repo`
    entry #owner   the user or organization which owns the repository
    entry #name   the repository's name
    entry #text   the repository as `owner/repo`
  entry .Asset   one release asset, as the releases API describes it
    entry #name   the asset's file name
    entry #url   the asset's download URL, read from the JSON field `browser_download_url`
    entry #digest   the asset's digest, `sha256:<hex>`, or `Unset` for an asset uploaded before GitHub computed them
  entry .Release   one release of a repository, with its assets
    entry #assets   the release's assets
  entry .indexReleases   indexes a list of releases' `.jar` assets by their SHA-256
      Pure, so it needs no network. Every asset whose name ends in .jar and whose digest is a
      SHA-256 is keyed by the lowercase hex; other assets are skipped. The API lists the newest
      release first, so where one hash appears in several releases the first, newest URL wins.
  entry .releases   lists every release of a repository through the GitHub API
      Pages through the API a hundred releases at a time until a page comes back empty. An
      optional token is sent as a bearer token. It logs `DepsEvent.Indexing` and raises
      `Repackager.UserError` for an HTTP error, a failure to connect, or a response which is
      not the expected JSON.
  entry .index   one index, from SHA-256 to download URL, across all the hinted repositories
      Lists and indexes each repository in turn, logging `DepsEvent.Indexed` with the number
      of assets found, and keeps the first URL for a hash found in more than one.

entry .DepsEvent   what Burdock logs as it resolves dependencies
  detail
      Every event is tagged `Log.Dependency`, and the two which make requests `Log.Network`
      too. `DepsDev.mavenUrl` logs `Querying` at `fine` and `Resolved` at `info`;
      `GitHub.releases` logs `Indexing` at `fine`, and `GitHub.index` logs `Indexed` at `info`.
      A `DepsEvent is Loggable` must be in scope; `logging.silentLogging` discards them.
  entry .communicable   renders each event as a message
  entry .Querying   a deps.dev lookup began: _querying deps.dev for the artifact with hash `hash`_
    entry #hash   the dependency's SHA-256
  entry .Resolved   a hash was resolved: _resolved hash `hash` to `url`_
    entry #hash   the dependency's SHA-256
    entry #url   the URL it resolved to
  entry .Indexing   a repository's releases are being listed: _listing the GitHub releases of `repository`_
    entry #repository   the repository, as `owner/repo`
  entry .Indexed   a repository was indexed: _indexed `assets` release assets of `repository`_
    entry #repository   the repository, as `owner/repo`
    entry #assets   how many assets were indexed

entry .ProgressBar   the repackager's progress bar: a forty-cell gauge in ember colours
  detail
      The drawing is ultimatum's gauge, with the smooth eighth-block bar, Unicode glyphs and
      the ember palette fixed; redrawing it in place is left to the caller.
  entry .width   the bar's width in cells: 40
  entry .render   renders a fraction of completion as a `Teletype` bar
      The fraction is clamped to the range from 0 to 1. An empty bar is all spaces, a full one
      all full blocks, and a fraction between cells ends with one partial block; the bar is
      always forty cells wide.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      ProgressBar.render(0.5)
      ```

entry .internal   the macro implementation behind `externalize`
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: the compile-time half of Burdock, written against the JDK alone since
      it runs in the compiler's JVM.
  entry .ResourcePath   the resource holding the dependency hashes: `META-INF/burdock.deps`
  entry .externalize   the macro: hashes and caches the classpath's JARs, writes the resource, returns the block
